      "topic": "Economy",
      "relevance": "Market-wide",
      "headline": "October GST collections rose 22.9% to ₹2.31 lakh crore, helped by tax on imports",
      "what": "Provisional government data released on Sunday 1 November put gross GST collections for October at ₹2.31 lakh crore, against ₹1.88 lakh crore a year earlier. GST on imports rose 42.2% to ₹72,246 crore and domestic GST 15.8% to ₹1.59 lakh crore; net collections after refunds were ₹2.03 lakh crore.",
      "why": "GST receipts feed the government's finances and give a monthly reading of spending in the economy. Tax on imports rises when import values rise, including the cost of crude oil, which India largely buys from abroad.",
      "source": {
        "name": "IANS",
        "url": "https://ianslive.in/gst-collections-jump-nearly-23-pc-to-rs-231-lakh-crore-in-october--20261101132850"
      },
      "also": {
        "name": "Bombay Samachar",
        "url": "https://english.bombaysamachar.com/business/gst-collections-october-2026-rise-23-percent/"
      }
    },
    {
      "topic": "Economy",
      "relevance": "Market-wide",
      "headline": "The Centre's fiscal deficit for April to September reached 42.1% of the full-year target",
      "what": "Government accounts released on 30 October showed a fiscal deficit (the gap between spending and receipts) of ₹7.14 lakh crore for April to September, 42.1% of the ₹16.96 lakh crore target for the year. A year earlier, the first-half deficit was 36.5% of that year's target.",
      "why": "The size of the deficit shapes how much the government borrows through bonds. Government borrowing feeds into bond yields, which affect the value of bonds held by debt funds and interest rates across the economy.",
      "source": {
        "name": "UNI",
        "url": "https://www.uniindia.com/business-economy/economy-fiscal-deficit-april-september/230762"
      },
      "also": {
        "name": "Trading Economics",
        "url": "https://tradingeconomics.com/india/government-budget-value/news/596809"
      }
    },
    {
      "topic": "Global",
      "relevance": "Market-wide",
      "headline": "Brent reached a six-month high after a tanker attack in the Strait of Hormuz, then eased on Monday",
      "what": "A tanker was hit by explosive devices in the Strait of Hormuz, reported on 30 October. The expiring December Brent contract ended at $117.64 a barrel, up 2.4%, and Brent gained 13.6% over October. On Sunday, seven core OPEC+ members kept December output targets unchanged; Brent eased to $111.84 in early Asian trade on Monday as Iran and Qatar held talks.",
      "why": "India imports most of its crude oil, so oil prices pass through to the import bill, the rupee, fuel costs and inflation.",
