  "learn": {
    "id": "L-003",
    "title": "Direct and regular plans: the same fund, different costs",
    "paragraphs": [
      "A scheme usually has two plans. A direct plan is bought without a distributor, for example from the fund house; a regular plan is bought through a distributor, and part of its expense ratio (the annual cost, taken from fund assets) pays the distributor's commission.",
      "Both plans own the same investments under the same fund manager, but the regular plan's higher expense ratio means its NAV grows more slowly than the direct plan's.",
      "From 1 November, AMC websites and apps show the direct plan as the default. Switching plans counts as a sale and a fresh purchase, so tax and exit loads can apply."
    ],
